12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the taxonomy of English modal auxiliaries, distinguishing epistemic, deontic, and dynamic modalities in science.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Modal Auxiliary System:) Tj
T*
(   Modal auxiliaries \(can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should, must, ought to\) do not) Tj
T*
(   denote physical actions themselves; they modify the lexical verb to express possibility,) Tj
T*
(   necessity, permission, or intention.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Three Primary Modality Dimensions:) Tj
T*
(   - Epistemic Modality \(Knowledge, Certainty, and Degree of Truth\):) Tj
T*
(   - Evaluates the likelihood of a proposition based on existing evidence:) Tj
T*
(   - Absolute Deduction: "The negative control produced a band; the master mix must be) Tj
T*
(   contaminated.") Tj
T*
(   - Cautious Possibility: "This conformational shift may facilitate substrate binding.") Tj
T*
(   - Deontic Modality \(Obligation, Permission, Directives, and Rules\):) Tj
T*
(   - Governs human behavior, legal standards, and institutional protocols:) Tj
T*
(   - Mandatory Requirement: "All researchers shall adhere to Good Laboratory Practice \(GLP\).") Tj
T*
(   - Prohibition: "Personnel must not handle radioactive isotopes without dosimeter badges.") Tj
T*
(   - Permission: "Students may operate the biosafety cabinet after completing training.") Tj
T*
(   - Dynamic Modality \(Inherent Physical Capacity or Capability\):) Tj
T*
(   - Refers to the physical properties, biochemical potential, or intrinsic traits of an entity:) Tj
T*
(   - "Thermus aquaticus can survive temperatures exceeding 80 deg C.") Tj
T*
(   - "This biosensor can detect femtomolar concentrations of troponin.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze modal hedging techniques in scientific Results and Discussion sections to calibrate empirical claims.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Epistemological Necessity of Hedging:) Tj
T*
(   Scientific discoveries are provisional and contingent upon experimental limitations. Unhedged,) Tj
T*
(   absolute assertions invite devastating reviewer criticism if slight experimental variations) Tj
T*
(   overturn the claim. Hedging modulates claims to match empirical validity.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Hierarchy of Modal Hedging:) Tj
T*
(   - Level 1: Categorical Fact \(Unhedged Assertion\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Protein A binds to receptor B." \(Claimed as absolute immutable truth\).) Tj
T*
(   - Level 2: Strong Epistemic Deduction \(High Certainty\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Protein A must bind to receptor B.") Tj
T*
(   - Level 3: Plausible Expectation \(Moderate Certainty\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Protein A should bind to receptor B under physiological conditions.") Tj
T*
(   - Level 4: Deliberate Scientific Hedging \(Calculated Possibility\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Protein A may bind to receptor B.") Tj
T*
(   - Level 5: Tentative Speculation \(Cautious Possibility\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Protein A could / might bind to receptor B.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Peril of the 'Double Hedge':) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
