(   Combining multiple hedging devices in the same clause produces weak, unscholarly prose:) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "These data might possibly perhaps indicate a potential binding interaction.") Tj
T*
(   - Revised: "These data suggest that a binding interaction may occur.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 7:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the syntax and semantics of past modal constructions \(Could have, Might have, Must have, Should have\) in experimental troubleshooting.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Architecture of Past Modals:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: [Modal Auxiliary] + have + [Past Participle \(V3\)].) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: Expresses retrospective evaluations, unrealized possibilities, or post-hoc) Tj
T*
(   deductions about past events.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Retrospective Deductive Analysis in Biotechnology:) Tj
T*
(   - Must Have \(High-Confidence Retrospective Deduction\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Because the non-template control amplified, the primer aliquot must have contained exogenous) Tj
T*
(   DNA contamination.") Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: All empirical clues point decisively to this past explanation.) Tj
T*
(   - May Have / Might Have / Could Have \(Past Speculative Possibility\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The low protein yield might have resulted from inefficient codon usage in the bacterial) Tj
T*
(   host.") Tj
T*
(   - "The cells could have undergone senescence prior to transfection.") Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: Represents one plausible hypothesis among several potential root causes.) Tj
T*
(   - Should Have / Ought to Have \(Unfulfilled Expectation or Corrective Duty\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The technician should have checked the pH before adding the enzyme.") Tj
T*
(   - "The culture should have reached optical density 0.8 by hour 24.") Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: The expected outcome did not materialize, or a mandatory SOP step was omitted.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 8:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the syntax of ditransitive verbs in passive voice, analyzing direct versus indirect object passivization.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Ditransitive Verb Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   Ditransitive verbs \(give, assign, grant, award, provide, send, offer\) govern two internal) Tj
T*
(   arguments: an Indirect Object \(IO, typically a recipient entity\) and a Direct Object \(DO, the) Tj
T*
(   entity transferred\):) Tj
T*
(   - Active: "The National Institutes of Health [Subject] awarded Dr. Chen [IO] a) Tj
T*
(   five-million-dollar research grant [DO].") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Two Syntactic Passive Transformations:) Tj
T*
(   - Transformation 1: Indirect Object as Subject \(Personal Passive - Highly Preferred in) Tj
T*
(   English\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Dr. Chen [Subject] was awarded a five-million-dollar research grant [Retained DO] by the) Tj
T*
(   National Institutes of Health.") Tj
T*
(   - Stylistic Note: Centers attention directly on the human scholar.) Tj
T*
(   - Transformation 2: Direct Object as Subject \(Impersonal / Material Passive\):) Tj
T*
(   - "A five-million-dollar research grant [Subject] was awarded to Dr. Chen [Prepositional IO] by) Tj
T*
(   the National Institutes of Health.") Tj
T*
(   - Stylistic Note: Requires inserting the preposition 'to' \(or 'for'\) before the recipient.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Diagnostic Rule:) Tj
T*
(   Both forms are fully grammatical; select whichever constituent represents the established) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
