(>> Solution to Question 9:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze prepositional passives \(pseudo-passives\) and the phenomenon of preposition stranding in technical prose.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Prepositional Passives Defined:) Tj
T*
(   In English syntax, prepositional verbs \(verbs followed by an obligatory preposition, e.g., rely) Tj
T*
(   on, account for, refer to, dispose of, agree upon\) can undergo passivization wherein the object) Tj
T*
(   of the preposition becomes the subject of the passive clause, leaving the preposition stranded) Tj
T*
(   at the end of the verb phrase:) Tj
T*
(   - Active: "The bioengineers thoroughly accounted for all thermal losses.") Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "All thermal losses were thoroughly accounted for by the bioengineers.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Preposition Stranding in Formal Biotechnology Literature:) Tj
T*
(   Unlike Latin or Romance languages which forbid preposition stranding, Germanic English syntax) Tj
T*
(   naturally accepts prepositional passives:) Tj
T*
(   - "The safety guidelines must be strictly adhered to." \(Not *to which must be adhered\).) Tj
T*
(   - "This anomalous band cannot be easily accounted for.") Tj
T*
(   - "Hazardous chemical waste was properly disposed of.") Tj
T*
(   - "The protocol was unanimously agreed upon by the oversight committee.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Editing Diagnostic:) Tj
T*
(   Never omit the stranded preposition in a passive construction; writing "*The guidelines must be) Tj
T*
(   adhered" is completely ungrammatical in English.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 10:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine ergative \(middle\) verbs versus passive voice in describing biochemical and biophysical state changes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Ergative \(Unaccusative\) Verb Class:) Tj
T*
(   Ergative verbs are labile verbs capable of functioning in two distinct active configurations:) Tj
T*
(   - Transitive Configuration \(External Agent Causes Action\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The researcher dissolved the lyophilized peptide in sterile water.") Tj
T*
(   - Intransitive Ergative Configuration \(Spontaneous Event / No External Agent Expressed\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The lyophilized peptide dissolved in sterile water.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Ergative vs. Passive Semantic Nuances:) Tj
T*
(   - Passive Voice \("The peptide was dissolved in sterile water"\):) Tj
T*
(   - Emphasizes an external deliberate human action performed according to protocol.) Tj
T*
(   - Intransitive Ergative \("The peptide dissolved"\):) Tj
T*
(   - Conceptualizes the process as an intrinsic thermodynamic property of the chemical entity) Tj
T*
(   itself, occurring spontaneously without human intervention.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Scientific Taxonomy of Common Ergative Verbs:) Tj
T*
(   - dissolve, precipitate, mutate, denature, freeze, melt, rupture, aggregate, dissociate,) Tj
T*
(   oxidize, proliferate.) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "When heated to 95 deg C, the double-stranded DNA denatures." \(Ergative - highlights) Tj
T*
(   spontaneous biophysical transition\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
