(   In active voice, 'make', 'let', and 'have' govern an object followed by a BARE INFINITIVE) Tj
T*
(   \(without 'to'\):) Tj
T*
(   - "We made the cells express GFP.") Tj
T*
(   - "The technician let the agar solidify.") Tj
T*
(   - "The PI had the postdoc sequence the clones.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Morphosyntactic Realignment in Passive Voice:) Tj
T*
(   - Passive of 'Make' \(Requires restoring 'to'-infinitive\):) Tj
T*
(   - Active: "The osmotic shock made the bacterial cells burst.") Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "The bacterial cells were made to burst by the osmotic shock." \(Mandatory 'to'\).) Tj
T*
(   - Passive of 'Let' \(Suppletive replacement with 'allow to'\):) Tj
T*
(   - 'Let' cannot be passivized directly \(*The agar was let solidify is ungrammatical\).) Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "The agar was allowed to solidify for 30 minutes.") Tj
T*
(   - Passive of 'Have' \(Suppletive replacement with 'instruct to' or 'ask to'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Passive: "The postdoc was instructed to sequence the clones.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 14:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the syntax and semantics of 'Must not' versus 'Do not have to' in biosafety containment levels.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Critical Deontic Polarity Contrast:) Tj
T*
(   A catastrophic misunderstanding occurs when non-native researchers confuse 'must not') Tj
T*
(   \(prohibition\) with 'do not have to' \(absence of obligation\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Two Poles of Deontic Necessity:) Tj
T*
(   - Must Not / May Not \(Categorical Prohibition - Action is Strictly Forbidden\):) Tj
T*
(   - Violation causes physical hazard, legal penalty, or contamination:) Tj
T*
(   - "Researchers must not mouth-pipette any liquid under any circumstances.") Tj
T*
(   - "Volatile toxic chemicals must not be handled outside a certified chemical fume hood.") Tj
T*
(   - Do Not Have To / Need Not \(Absence of Obligation - Action is Optional\):) Tj
T*
(   - The action is not mandatory; the researcher is free to do it or skip it without penalty:) Tj
T*
(   - "You do not have to wear full respirators when working in standard BSL-1 labs.") Tj
T*
(   - "Technicians need not autoclave glassware that is already certified pyrogen-free.") Tj
T*
(   - Hazard Warning: Writing "You do not have to open the hood" when you mean "You must not open) Tj
T*
(   the hood" could lead to fatal aerosol exposure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 15:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the dangling agent problem in passive constructions with introductory purpose or participial phrases.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Syntactic Anatomy of the Dangling Agent:) Tj
T*
(   When an introductory infinitive phrase of purpose \("To [verb]..."\) or participial phrase) Tj
T*
(   \("After [verb]-ing..."\) precedes a passive main clause, the grammatical subject of the passive) Tj
T*
(   clause MUST be the logical entity that performs the action in the introductory phrase:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Pathological Scientific Case Studies:) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "To assess microbial viability, the agar plates were incubated for 24 hours.") Tj
T*
(   - Linguistic Analysis: Syntactically, 'the agar plates' is the subject of the sentence, falsely) Tj
T*
(   asserting that the inanimate plastic plates intended to assess microbial viability.) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "After centrifuging the lysate, the supernatant was collected." \(The supernatant did) Tj
T*
(   not centrifuge anything\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

