(>> Solution to Question 21:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the syntax of 'Need' and 'Dare' as marginal modal auxiliaries versus lexical main verbs.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Dual Grammatical Identity:) Tj
T*
(   'Need' and 'Dare' function both as modal auxiliaries \(taking a bare infinitive without 'to',) Tj
T*
(   lacking third-person -s inflection, taking negative 'not' directly\) and as ordinary lexical) Tj
T*
(   main verbs \(taking 'to'-infinitive, third-person -s, and requiring 'do'-support\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Modal Auxiliary Usage \(Formal, Negative Contexts\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The technician need not autoclave pre-sterilized filter tips." \(Modal: no -s, bare) Tj
T*
(   infinitive 'autoclave', negated with 'not'\).) Tj
T*
(   - "How dare the investigator publish unverified clinical trial data?" \(Modal inversion\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Lexical Main Verb Usage \(Standard Modern Syntax\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The technician does not need to autoclave pre-sterilized filter tips." \(Main verb: takes) Tj
T*
(   'do'-support and 'to'-infinitive\).) Tj
T*
(   - "The cells need to be incubated at 37 deg C." \(Passive infinitive complement\).) Tj
T*
(   - Stylistic Note: Modern technical English predominantly uses the lexical verb form \("does not) Tj
T*
(   need to"\), while the modal form \("need not"\) persists in formal institutional directives.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
