BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the interaction between negation and modal verbs \(Epistemic vs. Deontic Scope of Negation\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem of Negation Scope:) Tj
T*
(   When the negative particle 'not' interacts with a modal verb, negation can attach semantically) Tj
T*
(   either to the modal itself \(Negation of the Modality\) or to the following main verb \(Negation) Tj
T*
(   of the Event\):) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Diagnostic Case Studies:) Tj
T*
(   - Case 1: You must not enter.) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Modal is positive obligation; negation applies to the event [Must + \(NOT enter\)].) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: You are strictly obliged to refrain from entering \(Prohibition\).) Tj
T*
(   - Case 2: You do not have to enter / You need not enter.) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Negation applies to the modal obligation [\(NOT have to\) + enter].) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: There is no obligation to enter \(Optionality\).) Tj
T*
(   - Case 3: The band cannot be the target amplicon.) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Negation of possibility [\(NOT can\) + be].) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: It is completely impossible that this is the target.) Tj
T*
(   - Case 4: The reaction may not proceed at 4 deg C.) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Negation of the event [May + \(NOT proceed\)].) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: It is possible that the reaction will fail to proceed.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
